            "text": "ROOT CAUSE:\nThe expression `(a + b) / 2` uses integer division because both `(a + b)` and `2` are integers. This truncates the decimal part before assigning the result to the `double` variable `average`.\n\nBEGINNER EXPLANATION:\nWhen you divide two whole numbers (`int`) in Java, Java throws away any decimal remainder. For example, `7 / 2` becomes `3` instead of `3.5`. Even though `average` is declared as a `double`, the division finishes as an integer first, and only then gets converted to `3.0`. \n\nTo fix this, divide by `2.0` instead of `2`. Because `2.0` is a floating-point number, Java will perform floating-point division and preserve the decimal part.\n\nPATCHED CODE:\n```java\nimport java.util.Scanner;\npublic class J09 {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int a = sc.nextInt();\n        int b = sc.nextInt();\n        double average = (a + b) / 2.0;\n        System.out.println(average);\n    }\n}\n```",
